# Native UIA lifetime crossover: offline assessment

## Evidence identity

The retained crossover is A-B-B-A in each configuration: A1, B1, B2, A2. Every report uses the same host `SINON`, AMD Ryzen 9 9950X3D, compiler 195136260, WARP 10.0.26100.9278, x64/X64, 96 DPI, 1280×720, 83 controls, 1,000 model rows, and five rounds of 40 frames. Within each configuration, `compare_performance.py` identity fields, `benchmarkSha256`, and all four `benchmarkInputs` hashes are identical across all eight reports. A and B executable hashes are stable within their respective repeated builds. A is commit `73ba9365726cce30299290ab9ae8afa065c4fe3d`, source-clean; B is `49c963f5751a6754e6b08e0362b96cfc7fc64414`, but its receipts say `sourceDirty=true`. The launch script's diff guard covered `src`, `include`, `Tests`, and `Build`; the global dirty marker remains a qualification limitation and is retained here.

The older baseline is commit `3022541ec86c28cfeef5e16c4c8224b5de73be5d`, on the same machine and exact benchmark identity/hash. It is useful as a resource context but differs from both A and B source. Its per-run comparator classifications are kept intact in `analysis.json`; all crossover reports also preserve their original `.comparison.json` flags against that baseline.

## Crossover readout

The comparisons show run-order and repeat variation of the same scale as several A/B deltas. In Debug clean, A FPS medians span 2,332–2,574 and B spans 2,221–2,619; the four B-vs-A pairings range from -13.7% to +12.3%. Debug dirty FPS pairings are -4.0% to +1.7%; allocation counts are identical at 10,280. In Release clean, the B FPS medians are lower than A in all four pairings (-1.7% to -11.9%), but A's own repeated FPS medians differ by 8.6%, the A1→B1→B2→A2 sequence drops then rebounds, and frame-P95 pairings range from -2.1% to +17.4%. Release dirty FPS pairings range -6.6% to -0.2%, while dirty frame-P95 changes split by A reference (-10.9% versus A1; +6.0%/+6.8% versus A2). This does not establish a stable timing regression or a clean non-regression. Flags are not waived: retain each report's `advice-required`/`within-noise-budget` status and the per-metric flags shown in `analysis.json`.

Allocation and surface results are stable across A/B within each configuration: composition allocations are zero, clean C++ allocations are zero, dirty allocations match at 10,280 Debug and 2,160 Release, and surface/replacement bytes are 3,686,400. No hidden preparations/composites were recorded. Compared with the older baseline, Release candidate working-set/private readings remain near or below baseline; Debug clean candidate private memory is about 1.6–3.5% over baseline and working set about 1.9–2.3% over baseline. A already shows similar Debug working-set elevation; the crossover does not isolate a new B-specific footprint. These are process counters, not GPU peak-memory measurements.

## UIA scope of this benchmark

The candidate production delta in `src/Controls/DxUi.Accessibility.cpp` adds control-lifetime weak tokens and pointer identity to semantic snapshot navigation records (around `AppendAccessibilitySnapshotNavigation`, lines 1228–1237), appends identity to generated UIA runtime IDs (`BuildRuntimeId`, around 3250–3283), and validates/captures snapshots for provider calls that previously refreshed only embedded targets (`CaptureSnapshot` call sites around 3937 onward). The new tests in `Tests/Controls/DxUiTests.Accessibility.cpp` exercise stale provider identity, same-path replacement, focus-callback replacement, and concurrent root replacement. The embedded benchmark itself does not create a WindowHost UIA provider or call UIA fragment/pattern APIs: `Tests/Embedded/ComplexUiBenchmark.h` creates `GraphicsFixture` and `ComplexUiScene`, then times `view.Prepare`/`view.Composite`; there are no UIA/snapshot/token references in that fixture. Although WindowHost snapshot building walks semantic controls, this fixture uses `EmbeddedView` and never invokes the WindowHost UIA snapshot builder. These runs therefore measure the unchanged offscreen WARP rendering workload, not UIA snapshot creation/traversal/token overhead. A possible module/code-mapping effect is not separately measured.

## Assessment

No stable regression is confirmed by this short crossover, but the flags remain actionable evidence and the Release clean FPS direction warrants preserving the candidate-vs-baseline question until any required advice or a quieter paired repeat is resolved. This is not a blanket performance pass. The report is descriptive: five rounds per process, two repeats per variant, global dirty marker on B, and process working-set/private counters limit causal conclusions. ARM64 qualification is separate.

Machine-readable run medians, all four pairings per scenario/metric, same-variant repeat deltas, exact original baseline flags, and SHA-256 inventory are in `analysis.json`.
